title: shell panel
tags: Panel [[Panel types]]
kind: reference
aliases: terminal output
summary: What the shell lines run in the workspace printed, read like a terminal: oldest first, one block per run, stderr in red, how a run ended.

Open it with `panel:open here shell` (<<key "panel:open here shell" "t s">>). A shell
line typed after `!` ([[Bash mode]]), a script started with `script:run`
([[Running shipped scripts]]) and a program started with `file:open-with`
([[Opening a file with another program]]) write their output here, not to the
[[message panel]].

It reads like a terminal, the oldest run at the ''top'':

* each run is a block headed by its command line and the time it started, followed by its output line by line, as it comes;
* what the run wrote on its standard error is in red;
* a run that failed ends with `[exit N]` in red, one stopped by `script:stop` with `[stopped]` (`[killed]` after `script:kill`); a run that succeeded adds nothing.

Two runs at once each keep their own block: a line goes into the block of the run that
printed it, not after whatever was printed last.

While the view is at the bottom it follows the output; once you have scrolled up into
older output, it does not move.

<<cmd "shell:clear">>, or the panel's ''Clear'' button, empties the log. Each workspace
has its own, and none outlives the session.

!! What stays in the message log

The [[message panel]] keeps a line `$ command` for each run, so that its history still
says what ran and when, and the status bar still reports a failure or a stop — and so
the message log. Only the output moved.

!! Why a panel of its own

The [[message panel]] puts the newest line at the top, which suits a history of
one-line messages and reverses a command's output: the last line of an `ls` came
first. Output is read top to bottom, grouped by what printed it — so it has the
reading order of a terminal, in a panel of its own.
